Z||000000||glava2876||09-18-2001||09:00 PM||||Well it has been a long haul, but after doing the fuel tank and pump, rebuilding the TBI and all brakes & master cylider,complete tune-up & wires etc., etc., the 13 year sleep is over and my 84 Fiero SE is on the road today! The only remaining thing to do is flush the cooling system. When I drove it around town today the temp gauge just barely moved and the fan never came on. The car ran fine, but there sure is a lot of heat rising out of the deck vents -is this normal? How do I verify the temp gauge and fan are working? I'm probably just worrying about nothing? Thanks, Glen||||reg||10
Z||000001||Master_Sushi||09-18-2001||09:25 PM||||I'm not sure about you question. But I jsut got an 84 fiero I've been restoring all summer driving last weekend. I took it for a short drive and im having coolent problums also. I'm going to try and sort them out myself, but I'll most likely be back here asking for help   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. It sure feels great geting a car runing after months of work. I had to drop the gas tank, swap roofs, fix some frame probs, redo interiour, and lots of other things I can't even remember. <P>I'm still not done yet either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif"> .<P>All in good time though,<BR>Good Luck!<P>EDIT-Brain no working goodly!...<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 09-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierohoho||09-19-2001||12:42 AM||||Glava, a quick check for the cooling fan is (if you have it) to turn on your air conditioner. Even if the A/C doesn't work it will run the fan. If the fan doesn't run with the A/C on I would use a test light to see if the fan is getting power while the A/C is on, I'll assume all the fuses are good. <P>I would also suggest a search in the archives, there is alot of stuff there on coolant fans. <P>I have an '84 also and the heat out the rear vent is normal. <P>Double check that your overflow bottle has some anti-freeze in it and that the rear cap has some visible when you take it off. <P>A small oil filter wrench is an easy way to remove and install that cap. <P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pennockpic.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||glava2876||09-19-2001||09:28 PM||||Thanks for the response. I'll try the AC test -great idea. I'm going to flush the system the weekend and will try and find the achive info -from what I have seen posted I know it is tricky. Thanks, glen ||||reg||10
